&lt;p&gt;If you’re comparing Profound vs. Scrunch, the buying decision usually comes down to where your AEO workflow stalls, not which tool tracks more answer engines. Both Profound and Scrunch measure how AI answers cite your brand, but they’re built for different jobs: Profound leans toward market analysis and content creation, while Scrunch leans toward technical optimization and task handoff.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This comparison article will walk you through the criteria that make them different: how many answer engines each plan covers, prompt capacity, reporting depth, technical optimization, bot-traffic tracking, actionability, agency and international fit, and pricing. Each section names which tool leads and why, so you can find the best fit for your team.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;I’ll also include &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo"&gt;HubSpot AEO&lt;/a&gt; as a third option, since teams already running their content and CRM in one place often want visibility data that connects to their publishing workflow. I’ll compare Profound vs. Scrunch first, then explain where a CRM-connected tool makes sense and where it doesn’t.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-whats-different-at-a-glance"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: What’s different at a glance?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#methodology-how-i-compared-profound-and-scrunch"&gt;Methodology: How I Compared Profound and Scrunch&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-which-covers-more-ai-engines"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: Which covers more AI engines?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-how-do-reporting-lenses-differ"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: How do reporting lenses differ?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-how-do-they-handle-technical-optimization"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: How do they handle technical optimization?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-can-i-track-agent-and-bot-traffic"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: Can I track agent and bot traffic?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-who-wins-on-actionability-and-workflow"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: Who wins on actionability and workflow?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-what-about-agencies-and-international-teams"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: What about agencies and international teams?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#profound-vs-scrunch-whats-the-real-pricing-path-to-value"&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: What’s the real pricing path to value?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-do-users-say-in-the-wild"&gt;What do users say in the wild?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#alternative-to-profound-and-scrunch-hubspot-aeo"&gt;Alternative to Profound and Scrunch: HubSpot AEO&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-run-a-low-risk-aeo-pilot"&gt;How to Run a Low-Risk AEO Pilot&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#frequently-asked-questions-about-profound-vs-scrunch"&gt;Frequently Asked Questions About Profound vs. Scrunch&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: What’s different at a glance?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Profound and Scrunch both track brand visibility across answer engines, but they optimize for different workflows. Here’s how they compare, with HubSpot AEO included as a CRM-connected reference point:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Workflow Takeaway&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Which platform fits depends on where your workflow stalls. Profound suits teams that want multi-engine monitoring plus automated drafting; Scrunch suits teams whose bottleneck is technical crawlability and developer handoffs; HubSpot AEO suits teams that want visibility, drafting, and publishing tied to their CRM in one place.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before you commit to a paid tool, run a free, one-time check with the &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-grader"&gt;AI Search Grader&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Methodology: How I Compared Profound and Scrunch&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;I wrote this comparison based on a mixture of personal testing of free trials, each vendor’s public documentation, dated pricing pages, and product materials, cross-referenced against verified 2025 to 2026 G2 reviews. All figures reflect plans available as of September 2026, with the source linked at each claim. I held Profound and Scrunch, plus HubSpot AEO as a third reference point, to the same criteria: engine coverage, prompt capacity, reporting depth, actionability, technical approach, team fit, and pricing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: #ffffff;"&gt;Implementation&lt;/span&gt; effort varies by site setup and CDN access, so treat setup estimates as directional. Answer-engine results are probabilistic. A single query proves little, so read visibility as a trend across repeated checks. Citation movement after a change shows correlation, not proof that the change caused it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: HubSpot publishes this comparison and offers HubSpot AEO, one of the three tools reviewed. I assessed all three against the same criteria and note where Profound or Scrunch leads.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: Which covers more AI engines?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Both Profound and Scrunch advertise wide reach, but the models you actually get depend on the plan you buy, so check the number attached to your tier.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Here is how supported engines break down by plan as of September 2026:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Profound no longer lists a paid self-serve plan for brands: its free Trial samples ChatGPT, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews for seven days, and coverage of up to nine engines is quote-only on Enterprise. Scrunch starts wider with four engines on its cheapest plan, but its full nine-engine lineup sits behind a custom Enterprise contract. HubSpot AEO tracks the same three engines on every plan: ChatGPT, Gemini, and &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/perplexity-the-ai-search-engine"&gt;Perplexity&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;More engines does not automatically mean better. Focus on the engines your target buyers use: Developers and researchers lean on Perplexity and Claude, enterprise IT buyers sit inside Microsoft Copilot, and mainstream buyers default to ChatGPT or Google’s AI results. Match coverage to your audience first, then treat total engine count as a tiebreaker.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before you trust a vendor’s engine claims, run three checks:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Confirm tier gating and add-on fees.&lt;/strong&gt; Confirm which models are included in your exact plan. Profound and Scrunch bundle engines into tier upgrades, so getting more models means moving up a plan, not buying them individually. Some tools (Peec AI, for example) sell per-engine add-ons.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ask how each engine is queried.&lt;/strong&gt; APIs, live-interface browsing, and user panels return different results, especially by region.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Separate sources from citations.&lt;/strong&gt; Only URLs a model links ba...

&lt;p&gt;Building an AI agent is a multi-step process. Marketers need to define the agent’s task, connect approved data and tools, and set limits on its actions. Once they master the basics, anyone — marketers, sales reps, or customer teams — can build agents with ease.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's Agent Builder" height="58" width="373" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Today, AI agent adoption is growing. A 2026 survey of 5,000 U.S. adults from Menlo Ventures found that &lt;a href="https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-consumer-ai/"&gt;one in four AI users&lt;/a&gt; has used an agent, with 24% of respondents citing regular use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To make the most of the tech, teams need to find the right use cases and start experimenting. This post covers how to build an AI agent for those just getting started. Discover how to deploy, test, improve, and set guardrails for your first AI agent.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;a href="#how-to-deploy-an-ai-agent-to-a-real-workflow"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;br&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-is-an-ai-agent"&gt;What is an AI agent?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#is-an-ai-agent-the-right-fit-for-your-workflow"&gt;Is an AI agent the right fit for your workflow?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-create-an-ai-agent"&gt;How to Create an AI Agent&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-build-an-ai-agent-without-code"&gt;How to Build an AI Agent Without Code&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#test-evaluate-and-improve"&gt;Test, Evaluate, and Improve&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#add-guardrails-and-human-handoff"&gt;Add guardrails and human handoff.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-deploy-an-ai-agent-to-a-real-workflow"&gt;How to Deploy an AI Agent to a Real Workflow&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#ai-agent-use-cases-for-marketing-sales-and-service"&gt;AI Agent Use Cases for Marketing, Sales, and Service&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#when-to-consider-models-protocols-or-multiple-agents"&gt;When to Consider Models, Protocols, or Multiple Agents&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#frequently-asked-questions-about-how-to-build-an-ai-agent"&gt;Frequently Asked Questions About How to Build an AI Agent&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#start-with-one-agent-your-team-can-rely-on"&gt;Start with one agent your team can rely on.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What is an AI agent?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An AI agent is software that uses an AI model to work toward a goal &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/autonomous-agents"&gt;&lt;em&gt;autonomously&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, or without human input. Agents can choose among approved tools, review the results, and decide what to do next until they finish the task or reach a set limit.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Agents need pre-defined goals or functions. For a marketing team, that could be to review a campaign’s performance and recommend optimizations. The agent would need to examine channel metrics, compare results with the campaign objective, identify likely causes, and explain the recommended change.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Looking to build your first agent? HubSpot users now have access to a no-code AI agent builder. Marketers can describe the automation they want to create, and &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/artificial-intelligence/ai-agents"&gt;Agent Builder&lt;/a&gt; will do the rest.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;AI Agent vs. Chatbot vs. Rules-Based Automation: Explaining the Difference&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/service/ai-agent-vs-chatbot"&gt;Agents, chatbots&lt;/a&gt;, and workflows have different problem-solving capabilities and serve different functions:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Chatbots respond to queries or conversations, answering questions that the user inputs.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Agents are able to make decisions on their own and problem-solve independently.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Rule-based automations perform a predefined task based on a set trigger. For example, if a buyer changes deal stages, an email may be sent with a discount code.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Agents, chatbots, and automations can also work together. A chatbot, for example, can act as the conversation window for an agent, as well as a predefined workflow can also include an agentic step&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;The Four Building Blocks of an AI Agent&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An effective custom AI agent needs a repeatable job, relevant data, a clear starting point, and a measurable result, as defined by Elle Oshiro, a professor at &lt;a href="https://academy.hubspot.com/"&gt;HubSpot Academy&lt;/a&gt;. These four building blocks help marketers turn an idea into a workflow they can put to use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h4&gt;1. Repeatable Work&lt;/h4&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI agents excel at regular tasks that need to be performed consistently. For example, a marketing team might review lost deals each month to identify buyer concerns. Even though each deal contains different evidence, the agent’s job stays the same: examine the concern, check existing assets, and recommend a next step.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That said, choose a task that the team can describe clearly and expects to perform&lt;em&gt; over and over&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h4&gt;2. Data Already in Your CRM&lt;/h4&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI agent development relies on high-quality training data. CRM data gives the AI context it can use to make decisions. Relevant information might include deal properties, company details, sales notes, and recorded customer conversations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before building an agent, check whether the necessary information exists, is current, and is accessible to the agent.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h4&gt;3. A Trigger That Fires Under the Right Conditions&lt;/h4&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A trigger determines when the agent starts its work. It could be a manual request, a schedule, or a change to a CRM record. With HubSpot, marketers and sales ops can use any of these options through &lt;a href="https://knowledge.hubspot.com/workflows/select-workflow-triggers-in-agent-hub"&gt;Agent Hub workflow triggers&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h4&gt;4. A Clearly Defined, Measurable Outcome&lt;/h4&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Define what the agent must deliver, what a correct result looks like, and how the team will measure success. For a campaign review, the output could be a report that flags underperforming channels and recommends one change to test. Each recommendation should cite the relevant metrics, explain the rationale, and acknowledge data gaps.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Is an AI agent the right fit for your workflow?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI agents are worth a closer look when a task is repeatable, tedious, and requires interpreting information&lt;em&gt; and&lt;/em&gt; deciding what to do next. If all you need is a summary of text or data analysis you paste into a chat, a single AI prompt may do the job. Even Anthropic &lt;a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"&gt;recommends&lt;/a&gt; starting with the simplest approach that meets the need, since agents can add cost and delay.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Three questions to ask before you build:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Where does the task requ...

&lt;p&gt;Companies without the internal resources or budget to build a dedicated search department still need to know what options exist for running AEO without a large team. Answer Engine Optimization is no longer optional for companies that rely on search to generate leads and revenue. AI Overviews now appear at the top of search results before a single organic listing — and sometimes above paid ads.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Three paths exist for running AEO without a large team. The first is a DIY workflow built around free or low-cost tools. The second is a complete software solution like &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo"&gt;HubSpot AEO&lt;/a&gt; that centralizes tracking and recommendations. The third is agency support for teams that want the work handled externally.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The right path depends on how much time the team can dedicate each week, what budget is available, and how consistently measurement can be turned into action. This article covers each path, walks through a minimum viable workflow for getting started, and provides starter prompts for building a repeatable tracking system.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-options-exist-for-running-aeo-without-a-large-team-when-deciding-what-to-do-now"&gt;What options exist for running AEO without a large team when deciding what to do now?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-options-exist-for-running-aeo-without-a-large-team-when-choosing-between-diy-software-and-agency-support"&gt;What options exist for running AEO without a large team when choosing between DIY, software, and agency support?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-options-exist-for-running-aeo-without-a-large-team-to-get-a-fast-baseline"&gt;What options exist for running AEO without a large team to get a fast baseline?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-options-exist-for-running-aeo-without-a-large-team-to-build-a-repeatable-workflow"&gt;What options exist for running AEO without a large team to build a repeatable workflow?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#frequently-asked-questions-about-running-aeo-with-a-lean-team"&gt;Frequently Asked Questions About Running AEO With a Lean Team&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#choosing-the-right-path-forward-for-running-aeo-without-a-large-team"&gt;Choosing the Right Path Forward for Running AEO Without a Large Team&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What options exist for running AEO without a large team when deciding what to do now?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Answer Engine Optimization requires measurement, prompt tracking, and content updates. All three can start in a single week without a dedicated specialist.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Four foundational elements determine how well AEO runs on a lean team. Each one is covered below, along with how AI tools can handle the execution work without adding headcount.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;1. Conduct a baseline visibility check.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The baseline visibility check comes first. It establishes where the brand currently stands in AI-generated answers before any strategy work begins. A useful baseline covers three things: whether the brand is mentioned at all, how it is described when it appears, and which competitors show up in its place. Without this starting point, teams have no way to measure whether later efforts actually work.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Two affordable approaches exist for getting a baseline:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ol start="1"&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Manually testing a handful&lt;/strong&gt; of prompts across core AI search platforms like ChatGPT, Google AI Overviews, and Perplexity, then recording the results.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-grader/"&gt;Using AI Search Grader&lt;/a&gt;, which returns a visibility snapshot in minutes without manual prompt testing across each platform.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ol&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI Search Grader is built for marketers who need a starting point without committing time or budget upfront. AI Search Grader requires no setup, no integration, and no dedicated tool spend. For a lean team, the value is speed — a marketer gets an initial read on brand visibility fast enough to fold into an existing workday, then uses that starting point to decide what comes next.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://blog.hubspot.com/hs-fs/hubfs/Frank%20H/what-options-exist-for-running-AEO-without-a-large-team-2-20261005-622358.webp?width=650&amp;amp;height=591&amp;amp;name=what-options-exist-for-running-AEO-without-a-large-team-2-20261005-622358.webp" width="650" height="591" alt="AI Search Grader dashboard showing brand metrics across ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Gemini with visibility scores and category breakdowns" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI Search Grader organizes brand performance into five categories.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sentiment:&lt;/strong&gt; The overall tone AI models use when describing the brand, evaluated across general tone, how that tone shifts across topics, and the credibility of sources shaping that characterization.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Presence Quality: &lt;/strong&gt;How substantively AI platforms discuss the brand, the quality of sources feeding that understanding, and the overall richness of available data.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Brand Recognition: &lt;/strong&gt;How widely the brand is recognized within AI training data.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Share of Voice: &lt;/strong&gt;How often the brand is mentioned relative to competitors within the same set of responses.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Market Position: &lt;/strong&gt;How the brand is positioned competitively compared to others in its category.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-grader/"&gt;AI Search Grader&lt;/a&gt; gives marketers a one-time visibility snapshot. &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo"&gt;HubSpot AEO&lt;/a&gt; supports ongoing tracking, which makes AI Search Grader a useful starting point for teams not yet ready to commit to a full monitoring workflow. To get the most complete picture, combining AI Search Grader with a round of manual prompts covers both the structured score and the raw search behavior.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;2. Build a starter prompt set.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A starter prompt set should include 15 to 20 prompts, organized into four categories.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Branded prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Direct questions about the company by name, testing whether the brand shows up at all when someone asks about it specifically&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Category prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Broader questions about the product or service category, testing whether the brand appears when a buyer hasn’t named it yet&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Comparison prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Questions that pit the brand against named competitors, testing share of voice and how favorably the brand is positioned&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Objection-style prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Que...

&lt;p&gt;When our blog team made the switch to an AI-first writing model in June 2025, my reaction was split. There was excitement on one side: I could see the inefficiencies in our process that AI might actually fix. On the other, anxiety. And while plenty of that anxiety was about the technology itself (how well these tools could actually write, how much we could trust them), my primary concern was my team.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;I’ve managed HubSpot’s blog writing team for three years, and before that was a HubSpot Blog writer myself. I understand what it means to have ownership over your process. Many of the writers I manage got into this work because they love writing. They enjoy the craft of finding the right sentence and earning a reader’s attention. Traffic numbers and conversions are secondary. During this AI transition, I felt the weight of what it could mean for those whose entire approach to their craft was changing fast.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's Agent Builder" height="58" width="373" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So much of the public conversation about AI content gets the story wrong. There’s an assumption that because AI can write passably well, the hard part is over. Just apply the same low-effort polish you’d apply to an AI-drafted email or memo to your blog post, then ship it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;But I can tell you from experience: it doesn’t work that way. A blog post is meant to compete against dozens (or hundreds) of other posts on the same topic. In this game, generic, low-effort writing will not win, and that’s good news for your content team. It means that even though the writing process has changed, the craft remains important.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So, how do you balance craftsmanship with the efficiency gains AI promises? And how do you build a process that takes advantage of what AI is actually good at without falling into its traps? We spent the last year figuring that out. Here, I’ll share what we learned: where time was saved, where it got added, and what it takes to make AI work without compromising the quality standards you’ve spent years building.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#before-ai-writing-was-personal"&gt;Before AI, writing was personal.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-we-learned-from-letting-writers-lead"&gt;What We Learned From Letting Writers Lead&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#directly-comparing-pre-ai-vs-post-ai-writing"&gt;Comparing Pre-AI vs. Post-AI Writing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#ai-content-at-scale-is-easy-ai-content-worth-reading-is-hard"&gt;AI content at scale is easy. AI content worth reading is hard.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-this-means-for-people-managers"&gt;What This Means for People Managers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#my-takeaways-as-a-content-team-manager"&gt;My Takeaways as a Content Team Manager&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Before AI, writing was personal.&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before AI, our writing process was, well, a writing process. While we had editorial standards and guardrails to ensure consistent quality, we didn’t enforce a specific method for how posts should be written. We hired writers and editors for their ability to craft, and we trusted them to use that ability. We understood that writing is more than just a technical skill. Giving our writers the space to develop their own individual workflows yielded content with personality and unique insights.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When training new writers, we taught a simple, flexible framework to help folks start to structure their time:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Outlining and planning&lt;/strong&gt; took around 30 minutes to an hour.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Research&lt;/strong&gt; could take one to two hours, depending on the complexity of the topic and the writer’s familiarity with it. This included sourcing quotes, finding original data, and writing from personal experience.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Drafting&lt;/strong&gt;, the part where someone actually puts words to the page, took around five to seven hours on average for a first draft.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Editing, QA, formatting&lt;/strong&gt;, and&lt;strong&gt; finalizing in our CMS&lt;/strong&gt; added another roughly two hours on top of that.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For a standard blog post, you’re easily looking at a full day’s work, usually longer. The bulk of that time lived in the drafting phase — writing the words, finding the right structure, and figuring out how to explain something clearly was the core of the job.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What We Learned From Letting Writers Lead&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When we formally adopted AI-assisted writing in June 2025, my approach was deliberate: don’t over-prescribe. Allowing our writers to develop their own processes had served us well for over a decade, and we wanted to maintain that sense of control for our writing team as much as possible, even as we added AI to the mix.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We gave writers access to &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-tools-grow-blog"&gt;AI tools&lt;/a&gt; (specifically &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/claude-vs-chatgpt"&gt;Claude and ChatGPT&lt;/a&gt;, along with NotebookLM) and space to figure out how to work them into their process. Even with AI, I believed writing is personal, and I wasn’t going to tell a team of experienced writers exactly how to do their jobs. Our goal was experimentation first, structure later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;What followed was not the clean efficiency win I expected. AI solved some problems and introduced new ones. But by working through those problems with our writers, we built a process that we know works.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Faster drafting doesn’t mean faster content.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;At first, the signs were good. Writers were putting words on the page faster than before, and I assumed that would translate directly into faster posts overall.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;It didn’t.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A couple of months in, after reviewing time tracking data and having conversations with my team, a more complicated picture emerged. The quantity of posts stayed about the same, but posts were getting longer, more detailed, and more demanding to produce. Writers were spending more time testing software, sourcing expert quotes, reviewing tools in-depth, and going deeper on topics. The time AI saved in drafting was being absorbed by everything else a competitive post requires.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;What made it harder was that some writers felt the pressure of both expectations at once: write faster and write more. When I understood that this was happening, I had to recalibrate. AI hadn’t decreased the workload. In many ways, it had increased the workload.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So my first lesson was: If you’re measuring AI’s impact on your team purely by post count, you’re going to be disappointed. The win is in what those posts become, not how fast they get there.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Research was our biggest surprise.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;I also assumed AI would make research faster. After all, instead of spending an hour getting up to speed on a topic, a writer c...

&lt;p&gt;The best Claude skills for marketing and sales teams turn recurring work into reusable processes with defined instructions, inputs, and outputs. Claude skills are reusable instruction sets for tasks such as campaign planning, sales call recaps, persona research, content outlining, proposal drafting, and performance reporting. Claude skills save marketers, sales leaders, and revenue teams from rebuilding the same instructions every time a task recurs.&lt;/p&gt;  
