&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What are Claude skills, and how are they different from prompts?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Claude skills are reusable processes that teach Claude how to complete a task using established instructions and resources. A prompt usually guides a single interaction, while a skill preserves a process that Claude can apply across similar requests. Claude skills help marketing and sales teams standardize recurring work without rebuilding instructions each time.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;What does a Claude skill contain?&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A Claude skill contains reusable instructions for a defined task. Skills can also include templates, reference documents, examples, scripts, or other supporting resources. A practical Claude skill usually defines the following.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Purpose:&lt;/strong&gt; The business task the skill handles.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructions:&lt;/strong&gt; The process Claude should follow.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Inputs:&lt;/strong&gt; Required information, such as campaign goals, call notes, or performance data.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Output format:&lt;/strong&gt; The expected deliverable, such as a campaign brief, recap, outline, or report.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reference material:&lt;/strong&gt; Approved templates, source documents, brand guidance, or supporting files.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Review requirements:&lt;/strong&gt; The parts of the output that need validation before publication, distribution, or a business-system update.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Claude’s skill architecture can also include optional scripts, references, and assets alongside the core instructions. These resources give Claude additional material when a particular task requires it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Claude skills also support developer workflows. A Claude Code skill can package instructions for recurring development tasks, including a Claude Code review skill that applies the same review criteria across code changes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;When is a Claude skill better than a regular prompt?&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A Claude skill is a better fit when a task happens regularly and follows an established process. Marketing calendars, call recaps, proposal drafts, and reporting narratives are common examples.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A regular prompt still works well for quick questions and one-off tasks. Skills become more valuable when teams need to preserve business rules, formatting requirements, source material, or validation steps across future requests.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Teams exploring broader Claude use cases can also see how HubSpot used Claude to &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/use-claude-to-create-virtual-assistant"&gt;create a virtual assistant&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Best Claude Skills for Marketing and Sales Teams&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The best Claude skills for marketing and sales teams support work that happens frequently, uses identifiable source material, and produces an output that can be checked before anyone acts on it. Campaign planning, sales follow-up, persona research, content outlining, proposal drafting, and performance reporting meet those criteria particularly well.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Campaign Brief and Calendar Builder&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A Campaign Brief and Calendar Builder converts campaign inputs into a defined planning framework. Campaign managers, content marketers, and demand generation teams supply the goal, audience, offer, channels, dates, and approved messaging, then use the skill to organize that information into a brief, timeline, and asset plan.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Campaign Brief and Calendar Builder skill flags missing audience definitions, unclear campaign goals, and unresolved deadlines so marketers can address those gaps before drafting continues.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Review focus:&lt;/strong&gt; Strategy, priorities, deadlines, and ownership.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/marketing"&gt;Marketing Hub&lt;/a&gt; gives marketers tools to execute campaigns and monitor performance once the plan is approved. The &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/claude"&gt;HubSpot connector for Claude&lt;/a&gt; can also support tasks such as checking page performance, creating or updating landing pages, and drafting marketing emails when relevant HubSpot context is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Sales Call Recap and Follow-Up Planner&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A Sales Call Recap and Follow-Up Planner converts meeting context into organized follow-up recommendations. Account executives, SDRs, and sales managers provide call notes, a transcript, and relevant deal information. Claude then returns a recap with action items, unresolved questions, and a draft follow-up.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The skill should distinguish confirmed prospect statements from recommended next steps. Salespeople still need to evaluate how the follow-up reflects the conversation, account relationship, and proposed next action. HubSpot’s guide to &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/sales/how-to-improve-...

&lt;p&gt;When we reviewed our Japan marketing automation programs last year, the picture wasn’t pretty. Our automated emails were generating about 12 qualified leads per month against a goal of 160.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That’s a huge gap.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We could have done the obvious and by hiring a local marketing automation specialist in Japan. Instead, we rebuilt Japan’s entire email automation program in two months, with zero additional headcount, using a model any marketing team can replicate. Here’s what we learned.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=b9960dd8-68b8-4caa-8a08-d3f7b7c3406e&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's Marketing Software for Free" height="60" width="502" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/b9960dd8-68b8-4caa-8a08-d3f7b7c3406e.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#the-problem-most-teams-dont-name"&gt;The Problem Most Teams Don’t Name&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#the-insight-behind-the-new-model"&gt;The Insight Behind the New Model&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-we-actually-built"&gt;What We Actually Built&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#the-benefit-of-targeting-high-intent-prospects"&gt;The Benefit of Targeting High-Intent Prospects&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#the-two-person-operating-model-in-practice"&gt;The Two-Person Operating Model in Practice&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-know-if-this-model-will-work-for-you"&gt;How to Know If This Model Will Work For You&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#the-playbook-briefly"&gt;The Playbook, Briefly&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-this-means-for-your-team"&gt;What This Means For Your Team&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;The Problem Most Teams Don’t Name&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;HubSpot is a US-headquartered company. And like most US-headquartered companies, our marketing automation investment has historically been concentrated in a handful of core markets: English, French, Spanish, German, Portuguese. Those programs get built, refined, optimized, and rebuilt again. Markets outside that core group tend to get less dedicated attention, not because anyone decided to deprioritize them, but because the automation resources weren’t scoped to cover them.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For Japan, that meant automation programs that were years old, partially built, or never meaningfully localized. The content existed, and the infrastructure was there. But it wasn’t performing because it wasn’t designed for the Japanese market.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This is a pattern that shows up across companies operating in multiple regions. Wherever there’s a primary market and a secondary one, the secondary market tends to lag. That’s because the people with automation expertise are almost always scoped to the markets that generate the most volume.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Marketing automation in markets outside the core group tends to fall into one of two failure modes: it was never built, or it was built years ago and never meaningfully updated.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Japan was the second case. And the typical response, “we need to hire a local automation expert,” wasn’t realistic. What we needed was a different model.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;The Insight Behind the New Model&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We were asking the wrong question. Instead of “Who can own this end to end?”, we should have asked, “Should one person own this?”&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Marketing automation has two fundamentally different jobs. One is &lt;strong&gt;technical&lt;/strong&gt;: building journey logic, setting up workflows, defining enrollment criteria, creating segmentation, and configuring tracking. None of that requires speaking Japanese. The other job is &lt;strong&gt;cultural&lt;/strong&gt;: making sure the message feels right, the tone lands, the pain points are real, the call-to-action makes sense. That absolutely requires someone who knows the market.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The mistake teams make is assuming both jobs need to live with one person. They don’t. When you separate them cleanly, you don’t need a local automation specialist. You need an automation marketer who understands the technical side, and a local marketing or sales partner who owns the cultural validation. Together, they cover everything.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What We Actually Built&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With that model in place, here’s what two months of focused work produced for Japan:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;What it took:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;~12 days of automation marketer time total (~1.5 days/week over 2 months)&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;5 days of regional marketer time for QA&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;0 additional headcount&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;What it produced:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;71 emails and 38 workflows built from scratch&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;2 core programs (one targeting prospects who hadn’t engaged with sales yet, one targeting leads already showing interest)&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Within the first reporting period, automation-sourced qualified leads jumped roughly 9x (from 12 to over 115 per month), making a significant contribution to Japan’s monthly qualified lead goal. We estimate that translates to about 44 incremental deals per month.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We deliberately focused on high-intent automation programs rather than trying to rebuild everything at once. Here’s why that paid off.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;The Benefit of Targeting High-Intent Prospects&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Treating all email programs the same kills most rebuilds before they start.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;There are two types of automation programs: high-intent and low-intent. High-intent programs go to people who are already showing early buying signals: they’ve visited pricing pages, browsed product features, or taken actions that suggest they’re actively evaluating. Low-intent programs reach colder audiences through broad nurture flows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The difference shapes a rebuild.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;High-intent programs have fewer emails, more focused messaging, and clear calls-to-action. They’re easier to localize, faster to build, and they convert. Low-intent programs have large email volumes, varied content, and much longer paths to conversion. They require significantly more localization work for a smaller payoff.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In Japan, our decision to start with high-intent trigger-based emails is the reason we saw 9x growth in qualified leads in two months instead of incremental improvement over a year.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;The Two-Person Operating Model in Practice&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Once we had one person owning the technical side and one person owning the cultural side, here’s how things played out day-to-day.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The automation marketer owned the following:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Journey design&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Workflow setup&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Segmentation logic&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Enrollment criteria&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Email structure and timing&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Technical QA, and reporting&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;None of this required Japanese fluency.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The regional marketing partner owned the following:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Validating that messaging felt right for Japanese audiences&lt;/li&gt; ...

&lt;p&gt;Transactional emails are automated messages triggered by a specific action a customer takes, sent to help that person complete or follow up on something already in progress. An order receipt, a password reset confirmation, or a shipping notification are all examples of transactional emails because each one delivers information tied to an action the recipient just took.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt; &lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=0c378c84-870e-41a9-88d5-e7ab7e7cb018&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's Email Marketing Software for Free" height="59" width="549" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/0c378c84-870e-41a9-88d5-e7ab7e7cb018.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That’s what sets transactional email apart from marketing email: a promotional campaign persuades a broad audience to take a new action, like making a purchase, while a transactional email responds to an action that’s already happened, delivering information the recipient needs rather than trying to sell them something.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Because transactional emails are expected, timely, and directly relevant to the recipient, they tend to see higher open rates than marketing emails. But that reliability only holds when they’re set up correctly: triggered at the right moment, formatted clearly, and compliant with email regulations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This guide breaks down what transactional emails are, how they work behind the scenes, and how to set them up across the customer journey from order confirmations to renewal reminders. It also covers compliance essentials and how to monitor transactional email performance.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-is-a-transactional-email"&gt;What is a transactional email?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#transactional-email-vs-marketing-email-whats-the-difference"&gt;Transactional Email vs. Marketing Email: What’s the Difference?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
